%n [harimtu | pīša | īpušam-ma]
#note: See @bib{RN117@316}: “a obv. 1-3. The text combines material found in the Pennsylvania tablet (OB II 51-5) with material that survives in the late version (SB I 206-8). Wilhelm thought the repetition of the phrase @i{kīma ili tabašši} unlikely, given the text’s terse style, but the repetition in obv. 5 of the formula introducing direct speech is another example of an elaboration present neither in the Pennsylvania tablet nor in the late text.”
// L I.4 OB "II" 51

%n damqātā-ma | [enkīdu || kīma ili | tabašši]
// L I.4 SB "I" 205
// cf. L I.4 OB "II" 53
// cf. L I.4 MB "Priv₁" 58

%n [ammīni | i]tti nammaššê | ša ṣēr[i | tattanallak]
// cf. L I.4 MB "Priv₁" 59
// cf. L I.4 MB "Priv₁" 64
// cf. L I.4 OB "II" 54-55
// cf. L I.4 SB "I" 206
// L I.4 SB "II" 29

%n [mannum-ma | kīma kâti || šaruh | in]a zikkarī
#note: See @bib{RN117@316}: “As suggested by Wilhelm, the line is restored after SB VI 173: @i{mannumma šaruḫ ina zikkarī}.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 173

%n alkam | enkīdu | [lurdēka]
// cf. L I.4 OB "CUNES" 5a
// cf. L I.4 OB "II" 56-57
// cf. L I.4 SB "I" 207

%n [ana kupri | ša rēʾî] | ašar (|) tarbāṣi
#note: See @bib{RN117@316}: “a obv. 6. Where the Pennsylvania tablet has Uruk and E-anna, MB Boǧ1 has the shepherds’ camp, which is the more immediate, if less inspiring, destination.”
// L I.4 OB "II" 75-76
// L I.4 MB "Priv₁" 78
// L I.4 SB "II" 37

%n ištēnšu | išhuṭ-ma | šū | ittalbaš
#note: See @bib{RN117@316}: “a obv. 6. Where the Pennsylvania tablet has Uruk and E-anna, MB Boǧ1 has the shepherds’ camp, which is the more immediate, if less inspiring, destination. The two lines that describe the clothing of Enkidu again have elements in common with both the Pennsylvania tablet (OB II 69-72: @i{išḫuṭ libšam ištīnam ulabbissu / libšam šaniʾam šī ittalbaš}) and the late text (SB II 34-5: @i{ištēn lubūšu r}[@i{amānša labšat}?] / @i{u šanâ lubūšu šâ}[@i{šu ulabbiš}?].”
// cf. L I.4 OB "II" 69-70
// cf. L I.4 MB "Priv₁" 73
// cf. L I.4 SB "II" 34

%n [libša | šanâ || ramānša | labšat?]
// cf. L I.4 OB "II" 71-72
// cf. L I.4 MB "Priv₁" 74
// cf. L I.4 SB "II" 35

%n [qāssu | ṣabt]at-ma | kīma ilim | pānīšu [illak?]
#note: See @bib{RN117@316}: “a obv. 7-8. Given the certain restoration of [@i{qāssu ṣab}]@i{tat }at the beginning of the line, one should probably resist the idiom @i{pānī ṣabātu} and take @i{pá-ni-šu} as @i{pāniššu}, locative-terminative with @i{alāku}. This phrase replaces OB @i{ireddēšu}.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "II" 73-74
// cf. L I.4 MB "Priv₁" 77
// cf. L I.4 SB "II" 36

%n [ana kupri | ša rēʾî | aša]r? (|) tarbāṣi
// L I.4 OB "II" 75-76
// cf. L I.4 SB "II" 37

%n [ina ṣērīšu | iphurū | rēʾû]
// cf. L I.4 OB "II" 77
// cf. L I.4 MB "Priv₁" 79
// cf. L I.4 SB "II" 38

%n [ītammu? | umm]ānum | ana ramānuš
